{
  // One strictness flag is deliberately absent, measured rather than assumed
  // (2026-07-28, tree at 345cb701):
  //
  // skipLibCheck stays true despite measuring 0 errors with it off. Turning
  // it off makes this gate hostage to third-party .d.ts quality, so a
  // Dependabot bump could redden CI for a defect we cannot fix in this tree.
  //
  // noUnusedLocals and noUnusedParameters are deliberately absent, per the
  // tool-ownership policy in
  // docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-27-ts-strictness-design.md: BIOME owns
  // unused code. Both were briefly enabled in tier 4 and then removed. They
  // are exact duplicates of Biome's noUnusedImports / noUnusedVariables /
  // noUnusedFunctionParameters -- same findings, no extra detection, two
  // error messages per defect -- and Biome names the three cases separately
  // where tsc emits one TS6133 for all of them.
  //
  // The original justification ("different gate legs, so a misconfiguration
  // of one cannot silently drop the check") did not survive scrutiny: both
  // legs run inside `make check-ts`. Nor does autofix favour tsc's removal --
  // Biome's fixes here are classified Unsafe, so `biome check --write` does
  // not apply them either.
  //
  // noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature is enabled as of tier 4. It required
  // turning Biome's complexity/useLiteralKeys OFF (biome.json), because the
  // two rules are exact inverses over the same expression: Biome rewrites
  // `rec["mode"]` back to `rec.mode`, which is what this flag rejects on an
  // index signature. They cannot both be satisfied. tsc's rule carries
  // information -- it makes "this key may not exist" visually distinct from
  // "this property is declared" -- and Biome's is purely cosmetic, so
  // Biome's is the one that goes. The disagreement is total and stylistic,
  // not site-specific: with useLiteralKeys on, every honest bracket access
  // in the tree (35 of them) emits a FIXABLE diagnostic telling the next
  // reader to undo it. Note biome.json is strict JSON -- a comment there
  // makes Biome silently discard the WHOLE config and lint with defaults --
  // which is why this note lives here.
  //
  // exactOptionalPropertyTypes is enabled as of tier 4. It distinguishes
  // "key absent" from "key present with value undefined", which is a real
  // distinction wherever a target declares `x?: T`. 27 sites in our code
  // (77 more are vendored and filtered). Three shapes of fix, and which one
  // applies is decided by WHO OWNS THE TARGET, not by taste:
  //
  //   - Target is vendored or third-party => omit the key, via a conditional
  //     spread `{...(v !== undefined && { k: v })}`. Widening is not
  //     available, because the rejection happens at the target's
  //     declaration. Note this means widening OUR OWN forwarding wrapper's
  //     props does nothing at all (measured on src/ui/TextInput.tsx).
  //   - Target is ours and an explicit undefined genuinely means "absent"
  //     => widen it to `x?: T | undefined`. One widening at a shared
  //     declaration beats a conditional spread at every call site, and in
  //     JSX there is no tidy way to omit a prop.
  //   - Neither end is ours => see src/ui/icons.tsx. @untitledui/icons
  //     redeclares `color?: string` over React's `color?: string |
  //     undefined`, so its `FC<Props>` fails the contravariant check against
  //     the vendored input's `icon?: ComponentType<HTMLAttributes<...>>`.
  //     Resolved with a real adapter component, not a cast.
  //
  // noUncheckedIndexedAccess is enabled as of tier 4, for src/ ONLY. Test
  // files are exempt from THIS ONE FLAG (and nothing else) via a deferral
  // block in scripts/typecheck_web.sh; the remaining sites and the exit
  // condition are tracked in todo/ts-nuia-test-sites-burndown.md.
  //
  // The split is measured, not a concession to volume. Task 4 cleared all 72
  // src/ sites and scored each: 3 were genuine latent fragilities, 69 were
  // the type checker failing to see an invariant a reader confirms in
  // seconds. The 3 share a shape -- a positional read crossing a structure or
  // function boundary (lanes[i] paired with spans[i] across a function call,
  // targets[i] with bucket[i] held together only by construction order). Where
  // no boundary is crossed the flag found nothing: topo/measure.ts scored 0
  // in 29 sites because every read sat inside the function that established
  // the invariant. Scored the same way, the test tree yielded two files worth
  // fixing (toporouting.test.ts, health.test.ts, 56 sites) and 305 that are
  // fixture[0].foo three lines below the literal that built the fixture.
  //
  // Deferring by SITE COUNT would have inverted that: the two biggest test
  // files, exportdoc.test.ts and chartoptions.test.ts, are exactly the ones
  // not worth touching.
  //
  // Every gate that reads this file must therefore go through that filter:
  // `npm run build`'s tsc leg does, and `make check-ts` does. `npm run
  // typecheck` deliberately does NOT -- it is the raw, ad-hoc view, and it
  // will report the vendored button.tsx error.
  //
  // =====================================================================
  // BIOME RULE DECISIONS -- tier 5 task 1: `correctness` + `security`.
  //
  // biome.json is strict JSON and cannot carry comments: a `//` there does
  // NOT error -- Biome silently discards the ENTIRE config and lints with
  // defaults, so the vendored exclusions vanish (180 files clean becomes
  // ~409 files / ~924 errors, with nothing saying the config was ignored).
  // Every rule decision in biome.json is therefore recorded here, keyed by
  // rule name. Later tier-5 tasks append to this block.
  //
  // Counts measured 2026-07-28 at 9a6193b8 with preset:"all" and domains
  // {react,test,types,tailwind}:"all": 636 diagnostics over 9 firing rules
  // across these two groups.
  //
  // OFF -- correctness/useImportExtensions (529 = 469 relative + 60 `@/`).
  //   The rule asks for the TRUE source extension (`.tsx`/`.ts`), not `.js`
  //   -- `forceJsExtensions` defaults false -- so tsc would accept the fix
  //   (`allowImportingTsExtensions` is already on). Declined anyway: the
  //   benefit it claims is resolution of UNBUNDLED ESM in a browser, and
  //   nothing here is ever served unbundled -- Vite bundles for prod and
  //   rewrites specifiers in dev. Meanwhile the vendored Untitled UI tree
  //   (96 relative imports, 0 with extensions, never hand-editable) stays
  //   extensionless, so adopting the rule splits the tree's import style
  //   along a line no gate can hold.
  //
  // OFF -- correctness/noUnresolvedImports (6). All six are the rule's own
  //   resolver failing, not unresolved imports: "react has no export named
  //   StrictMode/Children/Fragment" (x4, src/main.tsx, src/covapp/main.tsx,
  //   src/ui/CodeView.tsx) and, in scripts/e2e_coverage_report.mjs,
  //   "@bcoe/v8-coverage cannot be resolved" plus "monocart-coverage-reports
  //   has no export named CoverageReport". Node imports all six successfully
  //   (verified by running the imports), and tsc resolves the four src/ ones.
  //   Tool-ownership rules 4 and 5 in
  //   docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-27-ts-strictness-design.md: on a
  //   contradiction the tool carrying type information wins, so tsc keeps
  //   module resolution and Biome's restatement of it goes.
  //
  // KEPT, RETUNED -- security/noSecrets, entropyThreshold 50 (7 -> 0). All 7
  //   default-threshold hits were read individually and none is a
  //   credential: six are CSS selector strings ('[data-type="second"]',
  //   "div[class*='ring-1']", '[role="menuitem"]', 'div[style*="background"]',
  //   the shortcut guard's selector list, and a "#/coverage?ticket=GHOST-1"
  //   URL hash) and one is a vitest it.each title template
  //   ("formatOutage($ms) === $text"). Note src/ui/shortcuts.ts is SHIPPED
  //   code, not a fixture. 50 is measured, not picked: 41 (default) flags 7,
  //   45-49 still flag RunsPage.test.tsx's 'div[style*="background"]', 50
  //   flags none. Against 7 planted real-secret shapes the retuned rule still
  //   catches 6 (GitHub PAT, AWS key id, OpenAI key, Slack bot token, RSA
  //   private key header, JWT); only a bare AWS secret access key drops out,
  //   and that one already drops at 45. So keeping the rule at 50 detects
  //   strictly more than the alternative of turning it off -- and this repo
  //   has no other secret-scanning gate (no gitleaks/detect-secrets).
  //
  // KEPT, SCOPED -- correctness/noNodejsModules (65 -> 0), via biome.json's
  //   `overrides`. Every one of the 65 is a legitimate Node context: 20 test
  //   files, scripts/e2e_coverage_report.mjs, and the two vite configs. ZERO
  //   are in browser-shipped src/**. The override exempts exactly the files
  //   that never enter a bundle, so the rule stays a live tripwire on
  //   everything that does. Proven load-bearing: deleting the overrides block
  //   makes 66 reappear (the 65 plus the node:process import added below).
  //
  // KEPT, SCOPED -- correctness/useUniqueElementIds (18 -> 0), via
  //   `excludedComponents: ["Item", "Tab", "KeyRow"]`. Not one of the 18 is a
  //   DOM id: they are react-aria-components collection Keys on
  //   `Dropdown.Item` (which extends AriaMenuItemProps) and `Tab`, plus our
  //   own `KeyRow`, which forwards `id` straight through to Dropdown.Item.
  //   They must be STATIC -- ViewSwitcher's `selectedKey` matches
  //   id="topology"/id="hosts" -- so the rule's prescribed useId() fix would
  //   break tab selection. Biome rejects dotted names in this option
  //   ("'excludedComponents' does not accept values with dots"), so the entry
  //   is the member name's last segment, `Item`. The rule still fires on a
  //   real `<div id="...">`, proven differentially; the tree has 0 such sites
  //   today, which is why this is a tripwire rather than a cleanup.
  //
  // KEPT + FIXED -- correctness/useJsonImportAttributes (6). The six fixture
  //   imports now carry `with { type: "json" }`.
  //
  // KEPT + FIXED -- correctness/noGlobalDirnameFilename (3) and
  //   noProcessGlobal (1). Both are real, not style: web/package.json is
  //   `"type": "module"`, so vite.config.ts and vite.covapp.config.ts ARE ES
  //   modules, in which neither `__dirname` nor a bare `process` is a
  //   binding. They only ever resolved because Vite pre-bundles the config
  //   before evaluating it. Now `import.meta.dirname` (Node >= 20.11) and an
  //   explicit `import process from "node:process"`.
  //
  // KEPT + FIXED -- correctness/useSingleJsDocAsterisk (1), src/theme.ts. A
  //   false positive worth recording: the flagged line began with the `*` of
  //   the documented CSS selector `.dark-mode *));`, and the rule's UNSAFE
  //   autofix deletes exactly that asterisk -- silently rewriting the
  //   selector this comment exists to document. Fixed by rewrapping the
  //   comment (same words) so the selector sits on one line. The autofix was
  //   NOT applied; never run `biome check --write --unsafe` over prose here.
  //
  // KEPT, UNCHANGED -- correctness/useExhaustiveDependencies. Already
  //   "error", and it emits 0 unsuppressed diagnostics under the tier-5
  //   target config. The plan's "12 pre-existing hook dependency bugs" was a
  //   measurement artifact: its harness greps the rule name out of the whole
  //   report, and this tree carries 7 justified `biome-ignore
  //   lint/correctness/useExhaustiveDependencies` comments that Biome echoes
  //   inside OTHER rules' code frames. Anchor per-rule counts to the
  //   `path:line:col rule` diagnostic header, never a bare grep -- the same
  //   artifact inflates the plan's whole-tree baseline from 3,691 to 3,720.
  //
  // HAZARD for task 5's flip: do NOT put `next: "none"` in biome.json's
  //   `domains`. Measured here: with `react: "all"`, adding `next: "none"`
  //   silently disables correctness/useExhaustiveDependencies, and the only
  //   visible symptom is Biome reporting the 7 suppressions above as
  //   `suppressions/unused`. Omit the `next` key entirely.
  //
  //   CORRECTION (task 2): this entry also claimed `next: "none"` "swallows
  //   one suspicious/noEmptyBlockStatements". It does not. Re-measured both
  //   ways at 4d305c91: the `suspicious` group is 194 either way and the 90
  //   noEmptyBlockStatements sites are an IDENTICAL SET (diff of the sorted
  //   `path:line:col` headers is empty). Biome emits diagnostics in
  //   nondeterministic file order, so an unsorted diff of two runs shows
  //   spurious moves -- that is what the original claim measured. Compare
  //   diagnostic SETS, never two raw report orderings. The
  //   useExhaustiveDependencies half of this hazard is real and re-confirmed
  //   (7 `suppressions/unused` with the key, 0 without).
  //
  // =====================================================================
  // BIOME RULE DECISIONS -- tier 5 task 2: `suspicious`.
  //
  // Counts measured 2026-07-28 at 4d305c91 with preset:"all" and domains
  // {react,test,types,tailwind}:"all": 194 diagnostics over 10 rules, which
  // matches the plan's per-rule table exactly (the un-anchored grep artifact
  // that inflated task 1's numbers does not bite here -- no biome-ignore
  // comment in this tree names a `suspicious` rule).
  //
  // KEPT, SCOPED -- suspicious/noEmptyBlockStatements (90 -> 0), via the same
  //   `overrides` block as noNodejsModules. The plan expected "usually a
  //   swallowed error or an unfinished branch". Measured, it is 0 for 90:
  //   every hit is 47 arrow no-ops (`() => {}`) plus 43 class/object method
  //   stubs (`observe() {}`, `setOption() {}`, `dispose() {}`), and all 90 sit
  //   in 21 *.test.ts(x) files. ZERO are a `catch`, an if/else branch or a
  //   loop body, and ZERO are in browser-shipped src/**. An empty body IS the
  //   correct implementation of a test double -- the method must exist to
  //   satisfy an interface and must do nothing. So the rule is scoped off for
  //   tests and stays a live tripwire on shipped code, where an empty `catch`
  //   really is a swallowed error. Proven differentially.
  //
  // KEPT, SCOPED -- suspicious/noConsole (3 -> 0), same `overrides` block.
  //   All three are outside src/**: scripts/e2e_coverage_report.mjs printing
  //   the path it just wrote (that IS the CLI's output), and vitest.setup.ts
  //   capturing `console.warn`/`console.error` -- the console-interception
  //   harness itself, which cannot be written without naming console. src/**
  //   has zero console calls today, so scoping keeps the tripwire at no cost.
  //
  // KEPT + FIXED -- suspicious/useArraySortCompare (3), all in
  //   src/__tests__/topology.test.ts. Two are the real thing and the same
  //   class tier 4 found in charts/options.ts: `.map(e => e.parallelIndex)
  //   .sort()` over NUMBERS, where the default comparator sorts stringified
  //   elements. Both assert `[0, 1]`, which a lexicographic sort happens to
  //   get right -- a test passing for the wrong reason that would mis-order
  //   silently at [0, 1, 10, 2]. Now `(a, b) => a - b`. The third sorts
  //   strings, where the default is already correct; made explicit with the
  //   house idiom `a.localeCompare(b)`, which is also what the production
