  //
  // OFF -- suspicious/noEqualsToNull (37). `== null` here is deliberate: one
  //   test that catches null AND undefined, which is exactly what `??` and
  //   `?.` do internally. The rule's replacement `=== null` is NOT
  //   equivalent, and this is measured rather than argued: applying the
  //   rule's own autofix to a scratch copy of the tree (`biome lint --write
  //   --unsafe --only=suspicious/noEqualsToNull`, 18 files rewritten) takes
  //   tsc from 400 diagnostics to 428. All 28 new ones are "possibly
  //   undefined" / "possibly null", across 14 files -- charts/options.ts,
  //   data/{exportDoc,fragment,seriesIndex,topology}.ts,
  //   pages/{OverviewPage,SubjectPage}.tsx,
  //   shell/{EventEditor,EventsPanel,marking,SubjectHealthBanner},
  //   topo/{LinkInspector,nodes,TopologyPage}. Note the fix is classified
  //   Unsafe, so `biome check --write` will not apply it -- but
  //   `--write --unsafe` will, which is why this is an "off" and not a
  //   "leave it firing and ignore it". Tool-ownership rules 4 and 5: tsc
  //   owns null-vs-undefined and contradicts this rule 28 times.
  //
  // OFF -- suspicious/noUnnecessaryConditions (27). The plan called this
  //   "the closest Biome analogue to `ty`". Measured, its model of optional
  //   chaining is wrong: it evaluates `a?.b` as though the chain always
  //   succeeds, so it reports every `a?.b ?? fallback` as having a
  //   "guaranteed non-nullish" left-hand side. Reproduced in a self-contained
  //   file with no cross-module inference, `types` domain on -- it flags
  //   `line?.branches ?? []` where the parameter is annotated
  //   `{branches: number[]} | undefined` IN THAT FILE, `v?.a ?? "fallback"`
  //   where `v: {a: string} | null`, and `.exec(css)?.[1]` where `exec`
  //   returns `RegExpExecArray | null` per lib.es5.d.ts. Rewriting those
  //   three exactly as the rule prescribes gives 3 tsc errors out of 3,
  //   including TS2531 "Object is possibly 'null'" -- a null dereference. It
  //   does model noUncheckedIndexedAccess correctly (`xs[0]?.x ?? d` is not
  //   flagged), so this is specific to the `?.` short-circuit. 24 of the 27
  //   are that false positive; the other 3 (covapp/stats.test.ts) are `?.`
  //   after an `as` cast, where the cast is the smell rather than the `?.`.
  //   Tool-ownership rule 4 again.
  //
  // OFF -- suspicious/noLeakedRender (20). The plan called this "the
  //   `{count && <X/>}` bug that renders a bare `0`" and "likely the most
  //   valuable rule in this group". It cannot find that bug, because it is
  //   NOT type-aware: with `types: "all"` it still flags `{flag && <X/>}`
  //   where `flag: boolean` and `{obj && <X/>}` where `obj: {a: number} |
  //   null` (probed directly). It flags every `&&` in JSX whose left side is
  //   not syntactically a comparison. Typed by hand, the 20 split: 15 cannot
  //   leak anything renderable (11 boolean, 4 object-or-nullish) and 5 are
