  //   their `defineConfig(...)`, because that is how Vite loads a config --
  //   a named export is not read. The other two are `export default App` in
  //   src/App.tsx and src/covapp/App.tsx, the React root convention that
  //   all 7 of this tree's import sites already spell `import App from`.
  //   Scoping the two configs off would leave a rule whose entire live
  //   surface is the two app entry points, bought with a 9-file rename of
  //   an ecosystem-standard idiom.
  //
  // =====================================================================
  // BIOME RULE DECISIONS -- tier 5 task 5: the flip to `preset: "all"`.
  //
  // biome.json now has the .ruff.toml shape -- `preset: "all"` plus an
  // off-list. All 29 explicit "error" entries tasks 1-4 wrote were deleted,
  // each checked first against the resolved rule set (`biome rage --linter`,
  // see the tool caveat below) to confirm preset "all" enables it; all 29
  // were present. Three entries survive because their OPTIONS are the
  // decision rather than the level: complexity/useMaxParams (max 5),
  // correctness/useUniqueElementIds (excludedComponents) and
  // security/noSecrets (entropyThreshold 50).
  //
  // The flip changed nothing it should not: `biome lint
  // --max-diagnostics=20000 .` is BYTE-IDENTICAL before and after -- 180
  // files, 0 anchored diagnostics, 0 file-level diagnostics, 0
  // suppressions/unused, exit 0.
  //
  // DOMAINS -- the plan's domain block is wrong, and it is the tier's third
  // sighting of one trap. It prescribed nine framework domains pinned to
  // `"none"` ("records that they were considered"), minus `next`, which task
  // 1 measured as silently disabling correctness/useExhaustiveDependencies.
  // `next` is not special. Biome 2.5.5 declares FOURTEEN domains -- drizzle,
  // react, reactNative, test, solid, next, qwik, svelte, vue, project,
  // tailwind, turborepo, playwright, types (the plan omits `project`
  // entirely) -- a rule belongs to SEVERAL of them, and `"none"` on ANY one
  // of a rule's domains disables that rule outright:
  //
  //   qwik:      "none" kills correctness/useJsxKeyInIterable  <-- React's
  //              missing-list-key rule
  //   solid:     "none" kills suspicious/noDuplicatedSpreadProps
  //   next:      "none" kills useExhaustiveDependencies + useHookAtTopLevel
  //   turborepo: "none" kills suspicious/noUndeclaredEnvVars
  //   svelte / vue / reactNative / drizzle / playwright: cost nothing here
  //
  // An explicit rule entry cannot win one back: `"useJsxKeyInIterable":
  // "error"` alongside `qwik: "none"` still emits nothing (probed on a
  // scratch component with an unkeyed `.map` -- 1 diagnostic without the
  // domain key, 0 with it, either way the entry is written). A domain
  // "none" is not a default; it is a veto.
  //
  // So `domains` keeps only the four "all" entries, and the four framework
  // rules that actually fire go off BY NAME instead -- the same convention
  // as every other decision above, with the counts the domain "none"s were
  // hiding:
  //
  // OFF -- suspicious/noReactSpecificProps (456). Fires on every
  //   `className`, because Solid spells it `class`. This is React.
  // OFF -- correctness/noSolidDestructuredProps (121). Destructuring props
  //   breaks Solid's reactivity; in React it is the idiom.
  // OFF -- correctness/useQwikValidLexicalScope (82). Qwik's
  //   serialisation-boundary rule. There is no such boundary here.
  // OFF -- performance/useSolidForComponent (46). Wants Solid's `<For>`
  //   in place of `.map`.
  //
  // 456 + 121 + 82 + 46 = 705, which is exactly what preset "all" with no
  // "none" domain reports on this tree, so those four names account for the
  // whole of the plan's "~700 diagnostics". Net against the plan's block: 0
  // rules lost, 16 recovered, tree still 0 diagnostics. Twelve of the 16 are
  // vue/qwik rules that fire 0 here; the four that matter are
  // useJsxKeyInIterable, noDuplicatedSpreadProps, noUndeclaredEnvVars and
  // (via omitting `next`) the pair task 1 already saved.
  //
  //   THE TRADE, stated so it can be challenged: a domain "none" would also
  //   pre-suppress solid/qwik rules Biome adds LATER, which a by-name
  //   off-list does not. But that is the posture `preset: "all"` already
  //   takes for every other group -- a new rule appears, gets read, gets a
  //   decision -- and it is worth paying to keep useJsxKeyInIterable, which
  //   is a genuine React correctness rule that fires 0 today, i.e. exactly
  //   the kind of tripwire task 4 kept noNonNullAssertion for.
  //
  //   TOOL CAVEAT -- `biome rage --linter` prints the RESOLVED rule set and
  //   is how the table above was found, but it is a debug command and it
  //   misleads twice. (1) It PANICS -- "internal error: entered unreachable
  //   code: DomainFilter::as_rule_filters: domain tailwind not found",
  //   printing no rule list at all -- whenever `tailwind` appears in
  //   `domains` at ANY value, so every enumeration here was taken with the
  //   tailwind key removed. (2) With react:"all" AND qwik:"none" it still
  //   lists useJsxKeyInIterable as enabled, where `biome lint` does not emit
  //   it. Confirm every rage finding with a lint differential against a file
  //   that violates the rule. Rage is a lead; lint is the evidence.
  //
  // KEPT -- tailwind: "all", though it enables zero STABLE rules today
  //   (which is why rage panics on it: the domain has no rule filters
  //   registered). This is a Tailwind v4 app, so the opt-in is semantically
  //   right and costs nothing measurable -- the tree reports the same 0 with
  //   and without it.
  //
  // UNSET -- `project`. `project: "none"` would drop noPrivateImports,
  //   noUndeclaredDependencies, useJsonImportAttributes, noDeprecatedImports
  //   and noImportCycles. `project: "all"` adds nothing: rage claims it
  //   contributes noUnresolvedImports and useImportExtensions, but lint
  //   disagrees -- deleting those two "off" entries with no `project` key
  //   present brings back exactly 6 and 529, task 1's counts. Caveat (2)
  //   above, again.
  //
  // ADDED, BY NAME -- nursery/noFloatingPromises (2 -> 0). preset "all" does
  //   NOT include nursery, and the GROUP stays out: all 83 rules on measures
  //   3,356 diagnostics, ~80% of it from rules that are wrong here
  //   (noUndeclaredClasses flags every Tailwind utility, 1,949;
  //   noReactNativeRawText fires 204 times on a web app). Nursery rules are
  //   also renamed or promoted between minor versions, so naming exactly one
  //   keeps the upgrade cost to one line. Floating promises are caught by
  //   neither tsc nor any stable Biome rule, in a codebase built on
  //   streaming and chunk loads.
  //
  //   Both hits are in covapp/data.test.ts, the same shape twice: it calls
  //   loadFileChunk / loadTicketChunk for their SIDE EFFECT (the injected
  //   `<script src>`, whose URL-encoding is what the case asserts) and drops
  //   the promise, which then never settles -- nothing calls
  //   __OTTO_COV_FILE__ / __OTTO_COV_TICKET__ for those ids. Both are now
  //   marked `void`, which is the remediation the rule's own message names
  //   ("...or is not explicitly ignored using the `void` operator") and is
  //   writable only because task 3 turned complexity/noVoid off for exactly
  //   this interaction. Neither can leak a rejection: data.ts's
  //   _resetForTests() drops the waiter list rather than rejecting it.
  //   Proven live afterwards, differentially -- a scratch `work();` on an
  //   async function draws src/__fp_probe.ts:5:3
  //   lint/nursery/noFloatingPromises with the rule on and nothing with it
  //   off.
  //
  // NOTE -- `biome check .` formats biome.json ITSELF. A config that is
  //   valid JSON but wrongly indented (a script rewrite, say) fails the gate
  //   as `biome.json format`, which reads nothing like a rule decision.
  // =====================================================================
  "compilerOptions": {
    "paths": { "@/*": ["./src/*"] },
    "target": "ES2022",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noEmit": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "erasableSyntaxOnly": true,
    "noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": true
  },
  "include": ["src", "vite.config.ts", "vite.covapp.config.ts"]
}
